(define* (f-test-variance x y #:optional (alternative 'two-sided) (conf-level 0.94999999999999996))
  "F-test and interval for a ratio of variances, like R var.test().

Uses sample variance(x)/sample variance(y). Independent normal populations give the
F(df_x,df_y) reference; it is sensitive to nonnormality and is not a gatekeeper for choosing
Welch versus pooled t tests. O(n_x+n_y) work/O(1) auxiliary storage; inputs unchanged, no
omission/shared state.

Example:
    const auto f=dm::stats::f_test_variance({1,2,3},{2,4,6});
    std::print(\"{:.2f}\\n\",f.estimate1); // 0.25

References:
- https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/var.test.html

Arguments:
  X: At least two finite first-sample values with positive finite sample variance. List of real.
  Y: At least two finite independent second-sample values with positive finite sample variance. List of real.
  ALTERNATIVE: TwoSided (default), Less or Greater for population variance ratio relative to one. Symbol (one of two-sided, less, greater); default 'two-sided.
  CONF-LEVEL: Finite confidence level in (0,1), default .95. Real; default 0.94999999999999996.
Returns: statistic=estimate1=variance ratio, parameter1/2=sample sizes minus one, p_value and ratio confidence bounds; one-sided interval ends at zero or infinity. <hypothesis-test-result>.
Raises: datamunge-invalid-argument (std::invalid_argument) Too few/nonfinite observations, nonpositive/nonfinite variances or invalid controls.
