(goops:add-method! %g:dyadic-refine (goops:method args (core:apply %f:dyadic-refine args)))
(define (dyadic-refine . args)
  "Halves every knot span of a clamped basis (inserts each positive span's midpoint).

This is the nested-space step between consecutive levels of HB/THB splines: the refined
space contains the coarse one, and the operator maps coefficients exactly.

Example:
    auto r = dm::fda::dyadic_refine(dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(3, 5));
    std::print(\"{} -> {}\\n\", 5, r.basis.basis_size()); // 5 -> 7

References:
- E. Cohen, T. Lyche and R. Riesenfeld (1980). Discrete B-splines and subdivision
  techniques in computer-aided geometric design and computer graphics.
  https://doi.org/10.1016/0146-664X(80)90040-4

Dyadically refines every axis and maps the coefficients, giving an identical function on the finer, nested tensor basis.

Example:
    dm::fda::TensorBSpline f(dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(2, 4), {0, 1, 3, 2});
    auto g = f.dyadic_refine();
    std::print(\"{} {:.6f}\\n\", g.coefficient_count(), g.evaluate(0.3) - f.evaluate(0.3)); // 6 0.000000

(dyadic-refine BASIS)
  Arguments:
    BASIS: Clamped basis to refine. <b-spline-basis>.
  Returns: The refined basis and its refinement operator. <b-spline-refinement>.
  Raises: datamunge-invalid-argument (std::invalid_argument) If the basis is not clamped.
  C++: BSplineRefinement datamunge::fda::dyadic_refine(const BSplineBasis & basis)
(dyadic-refine TENSOR-B-SPLINE)
  Returns: The refined spline. <tensor-b-spline>.
  Raises: datamunge-invalid-argument (std::invalid_argument) If some axis basis is not clamped.
  C++: TensorBSpline datamunge::fda::TensorBSpline::dyadic_refine() const

See also: refine_basis()
See also: TensorBSpline::dyadic_refine()
See also: dyadic_refine(const BSplineBasis&)

Generic function with methods for <tensor-b-spline>; extend it with define-method. Other arguments call the module procedure above."
  (core:apply %g:dyadic-refine args))
(set! dyadic-refine (%documented dyadic-refine %g:dyadic-refine #f))

(define* (fit-tensor-bspline bases points values #:optional (options #:default))
  "Fits a tensor-product B-spline to scattered data by ordinary least squares.

Builds the sparse design B (each row has at most prod(degree_k + 1) nonzeros), then
solves (B^T B) c = B^T y. Coordinates outside a basis domain are clamped to it.

Example:
    auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(1, 2);
    auto f = dm::fda::fit_tensor_bspline({b}, {{0.0}, {0.5}, {1.0}}, {1.0, 2.0, 3.0});
    std::print(\"{:.3f}\\n\", f.evaluate(0.25)); // 1.500

References:
- C. de Boor (2001). A Practical Guide to Splines, revised edition. Springer.

Arguments:
  BASES: One basis per dimension. List of <b-spline-basis>.
  POINTS: Sample locations, each of length bases.size(). List of list of real.
  VALUES: One response per point. List of real.
  OPTIONS: Solver settings (default: automatic). <spline-solve-options>; default: the C++ default (#:default).
Returns: The fitted spline. <tensor-b-spline>.
Raises: datamunge-invalid-argument (std::invalid_argument) On empty or mismatched inputs, or non-finite data.
Raises: datamunge-runtime-error (std::runtime_error) If the normal equations are singular (some basis function has too little data; use a penalized fit or fewer functions).
C++: TensorBSpline datamunge::fda::fit_tensor_bspline(std::vector<BSplineBasis> bases, const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & points, const std::vector<double> & values, const SplineSolveOptions & options)

See also: fit_tensor_pspline()"
  (%f:fit-tensor-bspline bases points values options))

(define* (fit-tensor-pspline bases points values lambdas #:optional (order 2) (options #:default))
  "Fits a tensor-product P-spline: least squares plus an anisotropic difference penalty, solving (B^T B + P) c = B^T y with P from tensor_difference_penalty().

All-zero lambdas reproduce fit_tensor_bspline(). Positive lambdas keep the system
well-posed even with fewer points than coefficients (for order 0, or once the data
determine the order-q polynomial null space).

Example:
    auto b = dm::fda::BSplineBasis::open_uniform(3, 8);
    auto f = dm::fda::fit_tensor_pspline({b}, {{0.0}, {1.0}}, {0.0, 1.0}, {1e3});
    std::print(\"{:.3f}\\n\", f.evaluate(0.5)); // 0.500

References:
- P. H. C. Eilers and B. D. Marx (1996). Flexible smoothing with B-splines and penalties.
  https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1038425655
- P. H. C. Eilers, I. D. Currie and M. Durban (2006). Fast and compact smoothing on large
  multidimensional grids. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.csda.2004.07.008

Arguments:
  BASES: One basis per dimension. List of <b-spline-basis>.
  POINTS: Sample locations, each of length bases.size(). List of list of real.
  VALUES: One response per point. List of real.
  LAMBDAS: Nonnegative per-axis smoothing weights, one per basis. List of real.
  ORDER: Difference order (default 2, the conventional curvature penalty). Exact integer; default 2.
  OPTIONS: Solver settings (default: automatic). <spline-solve-options>; default: the C++ default (#:default).
Returns: The fitted spline. <tensor-b-spline>.
Raises: datamunge-invalid-argument (std::invalid_argument) On empty/mismatched inputs, invalid lambdas, or an axis with no more functions than `order`.
Raises: datamunge-runtime-error (std::runtime_error) If the penalized normal equations are singular.
C++: TensorBSpline datamunge::fda::fit_tensor_pspline(std::vector<BSplineBasis> bases, const std::vector<std::vector<double>> & points, const std::vector<double> & values, const std::vector<double> & lambdas, int order = 2, const SplineSolveOptions & options)

See also: fit_tensor_bspline()
See also: fit_pspline()"
  (%f:fit-tensor-pspline bases points values lambdas order options))

