(define (optimize self . args)
  "Maximizes `log_posterior` starting from `coordinates`, updating it in place to the mode found, and returns the log-posterior density there.

Minimizes `function` within [`lower_bound`, `upper_bound`], updating `coordinates` in place to the best point found, and returns its value. The initial `coordinates` (clamped into bounds) seed one member of the archive.

Run Adam from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Bias-corrected full-batch first and second moments, denominator sqrt(vhat)+epsilon. Tests the pre-update gradient norm after applying its update. No line search or general nonconvex convergence guarantee; O(p) reset local state.

Example:
    dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
    dm::optim::AdamOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
    std::vector<double> x{0,0};
    const auto value=dm::optim::Adam(controls).optimize(f,x);
    std::print(\"{}\\n\",std::isfinite(value)); // true

Run AugmentedLagrangian from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Uses fixed inner gradient steps on f+lambda*c+rho*||c||^2/2, capped so each displacement norm is at most one. Updates multipliers after each inner budget and doubles rho until feasibility tolerance or outer budget. No dual-stationarity test is exposed. Objective return alone is not a KKT certificate.

Example:
    dm::optim::EqualityQuadratic f({2,0},1);
    dm::optim::AugmentedLagrangianOptions controls;controls.max_outer_iterations=5;
    std::vector<double> x{0,0};
    const auto value=dm::optim::AugmentedLagrangian(controls).optimize(f,x);
    std::print(\"{}\\n\",std::isfinite(value)); // true

Runs the loop and returns only the best value.

Run CMAES from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Unbounded covariance-adaptation evolution strategy with weighted elites, evolution paths and global step-size adaptation. Default offspring count is 4+floor(3*log(p)). Covariance eigensolve is dense with numerical eigenvalue floors. Stops on search-scale/axis tolerance or generation budget; stochastic results depend on seed and population, not a global certificate.

Example:
    dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
    dm::optim::CMAESOptions controls;controls.max_generations=5;
    std::vector<double> x{0,0};
    const auto value=dm::optim::CMAES(controls).optimize(f,x);
    std::print(\"{}\\n\",std::isfinite(value)); // true

Run FISTA from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Classical t recurrence (1+sqrt(1+4*t^2))/2 and extrapolated gradient points, followed by the supplied proximal map. Stops on successive accepted-coordinate displacement. No adaptive restart, backtracking or monotonicity safeguard. Supply the correct proximal dimensions and a rate at most 1/L for the standard convex bound.

Example:
    dm::optim::L1LeastSquaresObjective f({{1}},{3},1);
    dm::optim::FISTAOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
    std::vector<double> x{0};
    const auto value=dm::optim::FISTA(controls).optimize(f,x);
    std::print(\"{}\\n\",std::isfinite(value)); // true

Run InteriorPoint from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Gradient steps on f-mu*sum(log(-c)) with c<0. Backtracks only for strict feasibility, not barrier-objective decrease, then decays mu after each fixed inner budget. Requires a strictly feasible start and can fail later when feasible steps underflow. No KKT or barrier-stationarity stop test; no phase-I feasibility search.

Example:
    dm::optim::NonnegativeQuadratic f({-1,2});
    dm::optim::InteriorPointOptions controls;controls.max_outer_iterations=1;
    std::vector<double> x{.5,.5};
    const auto value=dm::optim::InteriorPoint(controls).optimize(f,x);
    std::print(\"{}\\n\",std::isfinite(value)); // true

Run LevenbergMarquardt from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Forms J transpose J plus scalar damping*I and solves by unpivoted elimination. Accepts only strict reductions of half residual SSE, multiplying damping by .3 on acceptance and 10 on rejection. Tests proposed step norm even after rejection. A small rejected step is not a gradient certificate; no parameter constraints. O(np^2+p^3) work.

Example:
    const auto data=dm::optim::optimization_curve_data();dm::optim::CurveResidual f(data);
    dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardtOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
    std::vector<double> x{2,.5};
    const auto value=dm::optim::LevenbergMarquardt(controls).optimize(f,x);
    std::print(\"{}\\n\",std::isfinite(value)); // true

Run Newton from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Adds fixed damping to the supplied Hessian diagonal, solves with partial-pivot Gaussian elimination, and takes a full step. This is not a globalised line-search method; indefinite or nearly singular Hessians can increase the loss or produce huge steps. Stops on gradient norm. Dense O(p^3) solve/O(p^2) storage.

Example:
    dm::optim::QuadraticObjective f({2,8},{1,-2});
    dm::optim::NewtonOptions controls;controls.max_iterations=5;
    std::vector<double> x{0,0};
    const auto value=dm::optim::Newton(controls).optimize(f,x);
    std::print(\"{}\\n\",std::isfinite(value)); // true

Run SGD from the supplied point using the configured finite budget. Uses the mean gradient of each mini-batch, reshuffling component indices each epoch when enabled. Full objective remains the sum of components. Stops on absolute full-objective change per epoch. Nonempty components and positive batch_size are required; seeds reproduce the same build, not a cross-library shuffle transcript.

Example:
    dm::optim::LeastSquaresObjective f({{1},{1}},{1,3});
    dm::optim::SGDOptions controls;controls.max_epochs=5;
    std::vector<double> x{0};
    const auto value=dm::optim::SGD(controls).optimize(f,x);
    std::print(\"{}\\n\",std::isfinite(value)); // true

